c{10}{3} \\text{ units of juice}\n\\]\n\nTo make all four glasses have the same amount of juice, each glass should have:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{10}{3}}{4} = \\frac{10}{12} = \\frac{5}{6} \\text{ units of juice}\n\\]\n\n currently has \\(1\\) unit of juice, so Mrs. Jones needs to pour \\(1 - \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{1}{6}\\) units of juice into each of the first three glasses. The fourth glass currently has \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) unit of juice, so Mrs. Jones needs to pour \\(\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\\) units of juice into the fourth glass.\n\nThe total amount of juice Mrs. Jones needs to pour from the first three glasses into the fourth glass is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2} \\text{ units of juice}\n\\]\n\nThe fraction of a glass that Mrs. Jones must pour from each of the first three glasses into the fourth glass is \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\). The fraction \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\) is already in its simplest form, so \\(m = 1\\) and \\(n = 6\\). Therefore, \\(m + n = 1 + 6 = 7\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]\n\n================================================================ ==]\r\n================================================================ ==]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r\n�]\r