find the points of intersection. First, expand both equations:\n\\[\n(x - 4)^2 + y^2 = 16 \\implies x^2 - 8x + 16 + y^2 = 16 \\implies x^2 + y^2 - 8x = 0 \\implies x^2 + y^2 = 8x\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 + (y - 10)^2 = 100 \\implies x^2 + y^2 - 20y + 100 = 100 \\implies x^2 + y^2 = 20y\n\\]\n\nSince both equations are equal to \\(x^2 + y^2\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n8x = 20y \\implies 2x = 5y \\implies y = \\frac{2}{5}x\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(y = \\frac{2}{5}x\\) back into the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 8x\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + \\left(\\frac{2}{5}x\\right)^2 = 8x \\implies x^2 + \\frac{4}{25}x^2 = 8x \\implies \\frac{25}{25}x^2 + \\frac{4}{25}x^2 = 8x \\implies \\frac{29}{25}x^2 = 8x\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), multiply both sides by 25:\n\\[\n29x^2 = 200x\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to set it to zero:\n\\[\n29x^2 - 200x = 0\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx(29x - 200) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions for \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 29x - 200 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{200}{29}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x = 0\\), substitute back into \\(y = \\frac{2}{5}x\\):\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2}{5}(0) = 0\n\\]\nSo one point of intersection is \\((0, 0)\\).\n\nFor \\(x = \\frac{200}{29}\\), substitute back into \\(y = \\frac{2}{5}x\\):\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2}{5}\\left(\\frac{200}{29}\\right) = \\frac{400}{145} = \\frac{80}{29}\n\\]\nSo the other point of intersection is \\(\\left(\\frac{200}{29}, \\frac{80}{29}\\right)\\).\n\nThe slope of the line passing through the points \\((0, 0)\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac{200}{29}, \\frac{80}{29}\\right)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{slope} = \\frac{\\frac{80}{29} - 0}{\\frac{200}{29} - 0} = \\frac{\\frac{80}{29}}{\\frac{200}{29}} = \\frac{80}{200} = \\frac{2}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the slope of the line passing through the two points at which the circles intersect is \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\). The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]", "cimr_extracted_answer": "7"}}
