e, "raw_score": NaN, "norm_score": NaN, "cluster_size": -1, "alpha": NaN, "tau": 0.85, "method": "cimr_answer_only", "fmax": 0.875, "H": 0.543564443196711, "He": 0.18118814773223701, "M": 2, "r": 0.25, "n_clusters": 2, "judge_tokens": 603, "judge_elapsed_sec": 0.0, "fallback": false, "clusters_shown": [{"cluster_id": 0, "size": 7, "max_prm": 0.7852, "mean_prm": 0.4041, "agreement": 0.429, "modal_answer": "5", "n_variants": 3}, {"cluster_id": 1, "size": 1, "max_prm": 0.543, "mean_prm": 0.543, "agreement": 1.0, "modal_answer": "3", "n_variants": 1}], "judge_raw_response": "To determine the number of ordered pairs \\((a, b)\\) of integers such that the polynomial \\(x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 6\\) has 3 distinct integer roots, we start by expressing the polynomial in its factored form. Let the roots be \\(p\\), \\(q\\), and \\(r\\). Then the polynomial can be written as:\n\n\\[\n(x - p)(x - q)(x - r) = x^3 - (p+q+r)x^2 + (pq+qr+rp)x - pqr\n\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients with \\(x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 6\\), we get the following system of equations:\n\n\\[\na = -(p+q+r)\n\\]\n\\[\nb = pq + qr + rp\n\\]\n\\[\npqr = -6\n\\]\n\nWe need to find all sets of distinct integers \\((p, q, r)\\) that satisfy \\(pqr = -6\\). The possible sets of distinct integer factors of \\(-6\\) are:\n\n\\[\n(1, 2, -3), (1, -2, 3), (-1, 2, 3), (1, -1, 6), (1, 1, -6), (-1, -1, 6), (1, 3, -2), (1, -3, 2), (-1, 3, -2), (-1, -3, 2), (2, 3, -1), (2, -3, 1), (-2, 3, 1), (-2, -3, -1)\n\\]\n\nHowever, the sets \\((1, -1, 6)\\), \\((1, 1, -6)\\), \\((-1, -1, 6)\\), \\((2, -3, 1)\\), \\((-2, 3, 1)\\), and \\((-2, -3, -1)\\) do not consist of distinct integers. Therefore, the valid sets are:\n\n\\[\n(1, 2, -3), (1, -2, 3), (-1, 2, 3)\n\\]\n\nFor each set, we calculate \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n1. For \\((1, 2, -3)\\):\n   \\[\n   a = -(1 + 2 - 3) = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = 1 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-3) + (-3) \\cdot 1 = 2 - 6 - 3 = -7\n   \\]\n   So, the polynomial is \\(x^3 - 7x + 6\\).\n\n2. For \\((1, -2, 3)\\):\n   \\[\n   a = -(1 - 2 + 3) = -2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = 1 \\cdot (-2) + (-2) \\cdot 3 + 3 \\cdot 1 = -2 - 6 + 3 = -5\n   \\]\n   So, the polynomial is \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 5x + 6\\).\n\n3. For \\((-1, 2, 3)\\):\n   \\[\n   a = -(-1 + 2 + 3) = -4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = (-1) \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 3 + 3 \\cdot (-1) = -2 + 6 - 3 = 1\n   \\]\n   So, the polynomial is \\(x^3 - 4x^2 + x + 6\\).\n\nThus, the ordered pairs \\((a, b)\\) are \\((0, -7)\\), \\((-2, -5)\\), and \\((-4, 1)\\). There are 3 such pairs.\n\nTherefore, the number of ordered pairs \\((a, b)\\) of integers such that the polynomial \\(x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 6\\) has 3 distinct integer roots is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]", "cimr_extracted_answer": "3"}}
